      {
        "direct_properties": {
          "text": "**1. Lesion Description**\nLarge, well-demarcated, depigmented (achromic) macules and patches on the posterior neck and upper back. The borders are irregular. Within the depigmented areas, there are scattered, smaller, hyperpigmented macules.\n\n**2. Differential Diagnosis**\n*   Vitiligo (specifically considering segmental or non-segmental patterns)\n*   Pityriasis alba\n*   Post-inflammatory hypopigmentation\n*   Tinea versicolor (though typically presents with fine scale)\n\n**3. Suggested Next Information or Care Considerations**\n*   **Clinical History:** Duration of lesions, rate of progression, and presence of any family history of autoimmune conditions.\n*   **Physical Exam:** Check for involvement of other body sites, mucosal surfaces, or periungual areas.\n*   **Diagnostic Aid:** Wood’s lamp examination to confirm the degree of depigmentation.\n*   **Management:** Consultation with a dermatologist to discuss topical therapies (e.g., corticosteroids or calcineurin inhibitors) or phototherapy."
        },
