media-b72844b1-2b7e-4f98-a768-6f43e178da89", "status": "completed", "resourceType": "Media"}, "fields": [{"direct_properties": {"contentType": "image/jpeg", "url": "Binary/binary-b72844b1-2b7e-4f98-a768-6f43e178da89"}, "has_internal_children": false, "node_id": "400090", "labels": ["FHIR_ELEMENT", "BackboneElement", "content"], "field_name": "content"}, {"direct_properties": {"text": "Dermatology"}, "has_internal_children": true, "node_id": "400091", "labels": ["FHIR_ELEMENT", "CodeableConcept", "modality"], "field_name": "modality"}, {"direct_properties": {"text": "**1. Lesion Description**\nLarge, well-demarcated, depigmented (achromic) macules and patches located on the posterior neck and upper back. The patches exhibit irregular borders and contain scattered, smaller hyperpigmented macules within the depigmented areas.\n\n**2. Differential Diagnosis**\n*   Vitiligo (specifically segmental or non-segmental)\n*   Pityriasis alba\n*   Post-inflammatory hypopigmentation\n*   Tinea versicolor (though typically presents with fine scale)\n\n**3. Suggested Next Information or Care Considerations**\n*   **Clinical History:** Determine the onset, rate of progression, and presence of any associated itching or scaling.\n*   **Physical Exam:** Check for similar lesions on other body sites (hands, face, mucosal surfaces).\n*   **Diagnostic Testing:** Wood's lamp examination to confirm the degree of depigmentation.\n*   **Specialist Referral:** Consultation with a dermatologist for definitive diagnosis and management options (e.g., topical corticosteroids, calcineurin inhibitors, or phototherapy)."}, "has_internal_children": false, "node_id": "400093", "labels": ["FHIR_ELEMENT", "BackboneElement", "note"], "field_name": "note"}, {"direct_properties": {"reference": "Patient/12261"}, "has_internal_children": false, "node_id": "400089", "labels": ["FHIR_ELEMENT", "Reference", "subject"], "field_name": "subject"}]}, {"resource_id": "media-bcef95e3-e6d7-4a38-8383-c5fd3a6cb1ab", "resource_found": true, "resource_type": "Media", "root_properties": {"createdDateTime": "2026-08-04T02:09:13Z", "id": "media-bcef95e3-e6d7-4a38-8383-c5fd3a6cb1ab", "status": "completed", "resourceType": "Media"}, "fields": [{"direct_properties": {"contentType": "image/jpeg", "url": "Binary/binary-bcef95e3-e6d7-4a38-8383-c5fd3a6cb1ab"}, "has_internal_children": false, "node_id": "400049", "labels": ["FHIR_ELEMENT", "BackboneElement", "content"], "field_name": "content"}, {"direct_properties": {"text": "Dermatology"}, "has_internal_children": true, "node_id": "400050", "labels": ["FHIR_ELEMENT", "CodeableConcept", "modality"], "field_name": "modality"}, {"direct_properties": {"text": "**1. Lesion Description**\nLarge, well-demarcated, depigmented (achromic) macules and patches on the posterior neck and upper back. The patches exhibit irregular borders and contain scattered, smaller hyperpigmented macules within the depigmented areas.\n\n**2. Differential Diagnosis**\n*   Vitiligo (specifically segmental or non-segmental)\n*   Pityriasis alba\n*   Post-inflammatory hypopigmentation\n*   Tinea versicolor (though typically presents with fine scale)\n\n**3. Suggested Next Information or Care Considerations**\n*   **Clinical History:** Duration of lesions, rate of progression, and presence of any family history of autoimmune conditions.\n*   **Physical Exam:** Check for involvement of other body sites or mucosal surfaces.\n*   **Diagnostic Aid:** Wood's lamp examination to confirm the degree of depigmentation.\n*   **Management:** Consultation with a dermatologist to discuss topical therapies (e.g., corticosteroids or calcineurin inhibitors) or phototherapy."}, "has_internal_children": false, "node_id": "400052", "labels": ["FHIR_ELEMENT", "BackboneElement", "note"], "field_name": "note"}, {"direct_properties": {"reference": "Patient/12261"}, "has_internal_children": false, "node_id": "400048", "labels": ["FHIR_ELEMENT", "Reference", "subject"], "field_name": "subject"}]}, {"resource_id": "media-ec8e7575-3cb1-4e88-85df-ade3c96b4780", "resource_found": true, "resource_type": "Media", "root_properties": {"createdDateTime": "2026-08-04T01:40:37Z", "id": "media-ec8e7575-3cb1-4e88-85df-ade3c96b4780", "status": "completed", "resourceType": "Media"}, "fields": [{"direct_properties": {"contentType": "image/jpeg", "url": "Binary/binary-ec8e7575-3cb1-4e88-85df-ade3c96b4780"}, "has_internal_children": false, "node_id": "400030", "labels": ["FHIR_ELEMENT", "BackboneElement", "content"], "field_name": "content"}, {"direct_properties": {"text": "Dermatology"}, "has_internal_children": true, "node_id": "400031", "labels": ["FHIR_ELEMENT", "CodeableConcept", "modality"], "field_name": "modality"}, {"direct_properties": {"text": "**1. Lesion Description**\nLarge, well-demarcated, depigmented (achromic) macules and patches located on the posterior neck and upper back. The patches exhibit irregular borders and contain scattered, smaller hyperpigmented macules within the depigmented areas.\n\n**2. Differential Diagnosis**\n*   Vitiligo (specifically non-segmental)\n*   Post-inflammatory hypopigmentation\n*   Pityriasis alba\n*   Tinea versicolor (less likely given the degree of depigmentation)\n\n**3. Suggested Next Information or Care Considerations**\n*   **Clinical History:** Determine the onset, rate of progression, and presence of any associated itching or scaling.\n*   **Physical Exam:** Check for similar lesions on other body sites (face, hands, or mucosal surfaces).\n*   **Diagnostic Testing:** A Wood’s lamp examination may help confirm the loss of pigment.\n*   **Management:** Consultation with a dermatologist for potential topical therapies (e.g., corticosteroids or calcineurin inhibitors) or phototherapy."}, "has_internal_children": false, "node_id": "400033", "labels": ["FHIR_ELEMENT", "BackboneElement", "note"], "field_name": "note"}, {"direct_properties": {"reference": "Patient/12261"}, "has_internal_children": false, "node_id": "400029", "labels": ["FHIR_ELEMENT", "Reference", "subject"], "field_name": "subject"}]}]}
